ing seen his grandfather only once in  his life Clarence knew almost nothing  about the man who was suddenly to be his  guardian he regarded Myers Anderson is  some kind of God a rich man who lived in  a big house and owned both a car and a  truck he was utterly terrified even  before he crossed the threshold at all  man Anderson stood ramrod straight and  walked stiffly years of chopping  firewood his first business had chiseled  his big hands into callous knobs one of  the few photos of him taken much later  in his life shows a lean man dressed in  a white t-shirt Anderson's dark black  skin creased and weather-beaten almost  blends into the mahogany mantelpiece  behind him there's a trace of a scowl  across his face his eyes burned  defiantly at the camera and the muscles  in his body looked tense barely  suppressing the obvious resistance he  felt over being photographed Anderson  delivered heating oil to black customers  in green Ford truck painted letters on  the side advertised his business  Anderson fuel oil company the Anderson's  two-bedroom house built just before  Clarence and Myers arrived surrounded  the boys with luxuries they had never  known a secured roof over their head  an indoor toilet a bedroom of their own  Anderson bought them clothes in decent  shoes his wife Christine the boy's  grandmother made sure they had plenty to  eat  but the comforts came at a price under  Anderson's roof the boys lived by his  strict rules he allowed them no break-in  period and accepted no excuses he  immediately taught his grandsons to tell  time by his clock each morning he walked  before the Sun rose Foday he called it  and he worked from Sun to Sun he put his  grandsons on the same schedule rousing  them before sunrise every day even  during the summer the morning ritual  became so ingrained that Clarence often  sensed his grandfather's presence in the  pre-dawn darkness before he heard his  deep voice get up boy Anderson Bart  y'all think y'all are rich Clarence  Thomas cannot remember a single morning  of his childhood that he was not up to  see daybreak my other favorite story  about Myers Anderson is that the green  Ford trucks that he bought came  installed with heaters Anderson took  them out a heater was a luxury that  would make a working man lazy he  believed there's no question that the  discipline and the work ethic that  Clarence Thomas learned from Myers  Anderson is a big part of the reason  that he was successful in life but the  other point here is that this was a  childhood that was very isolated  Anderson used to say that if you weren't  working you were trifling and he didn't  allow Clarence or his brother to play  after school on the week or on the  weekends when there was work to be done  when you talked to Thomas's friends from  that time period  they remember him in two places sitting  next to his grandfather in his  grandfather's oil truck or in the  library reading Clarence Thomas was a  voracious reader from a very very young  age  and books were one of his few escapes I  point this out because Thomas is  something of a loner and this becomes  significant in terms of what happens  later in his life when he decides that  he is conservative and is going to be a  black member of the Republican Party  being a minority is familiar to him and  not that big a deal  Justice Thomas gave a speech in 2000  where he said I'm very comfortable being  alone in my views he is  the other thing you need to know about  Clarence Thomas is that he was raised in  the Catholic Church and he attended  Catholic parochial schools this was a  very very different experience than most  children of his generation fewer than 5%  of savannahs Catholic families black  Catholic are black families identified  themselves as Catholics and the Catholic  Church with its different rituals and  traditions reinforce the notion with  Clarence Thomas that it was okay to be  different and to hold contrary views  more importantly the Catholic Church  really saved Clarence Thomas the  parochial schools that the church ran in  Savannah offered the best education  possible for a black child growing up in  under segregation in the deep south at  that time and I'm going to read again a  little bit to give you a a taste of what  that experience was like for Clarence  Thomas the school that he attended  beginning in the second grade was called  st. Benedict's st. Benedict's a  three-story brick fortress about eight  blocks from Henderson's house  represented the outer edge of Clarence  Thomas's tiny universe in Savannah nuns  from the order of st. Francis and  priests from the Society of African  missions staff st. benedict's in  savannas to other Catholic elementary  schools dressed in the dark wool habits  of their order the nuns were an  intimidating force the first lesson they  taught was how to address them yes  sister no sister please sister thank you  sister they demanded perfect behavior  from their students Charles Bell a  classmate of Thomas's brother Myers can  still visualize the banner over the  blackboard in one of the classrooms at  st. Benedict's if you want to kill time  you work it to death  the sisters proud their classrooms in  the school halls with rulers ready to  wrap the knuckles of any misbehaving  student you did not have the freedom to  do what you wanted to do sin Pamela  Jones you had to control yourself that  first year the nuns unyielding  discipline almost overwhelmed Clarence  living in pinpoint and then with his  mother he had been mostly unsupervised  accustomed to roaming the streets on his  own even when he was supposed to be in  school his first year at st. Benedict's  he ran straight C's except for a B and  Christian doctrine Anderson's towering  expectations also pressed on him daily  as much as he believed in the importance  of manual skills Anderson was determined  that his grandsons not repeat his life  of hard labor he often told Larenz that  he wanted him to study